## Their Standing With These People **(synthesize)**

> Guidance: who the learner already is to the people whose speech they are learning, and
> what is new. Name the relationship and its history in plain facts (how long, how often,
> where). The most important sentence here is the framing generators must never get wrong:
> a learner who is already family must never be written as a stranger at the gate; a
> newcomer must never be written as an old friend. If they are new, say so, and say what
> the welcome is like. 3–6 sentences.

[Write the standing facts.]

## Why Now **(synthesize)**

> Guidance: one or two sentences on why the learner is starting at this point in their
> life — in their own words if they gave them. It is the premise, never ground to make up.

[Write the premise.]
